<h2 id="cookies">1. Cookies</h2>
<p>
  <strong>This site sets no cookies of its own, and no third party sets cookies through it.</strong> There is no
  advertising, no tag manager, no social tracking pixel, no Google Analytics, and no embedded video player.
</p>

<h2 id="storage">2. What is stored in your browser</h2>
<p>
  Three things may be written to your browser's local storage. None is a cookie, none is sent to a server
  automatically, and none is used to track you across sites.
</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th scope="col">Name</th>
      <th scope="col">Type</th>
      <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
      <th scope="col">Lifetime</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>theme</code></td>
      <td><code>localStorage</code></td>
      <td>Remembers whether you chose the light or dark colour scheme. Written only when you use the theme toggle.</td>
      <td>Until you clear your browser data.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>analytics-consent</code></td>
      <td><code>localStorage</code></td>
      <td>Remembers whether you accepted or declined the page-view counter, so you are not asked again.</td>
      <td>Until you clear your browser data.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>firebaseLocalStorageDb</code></td>
      <td><code>IndexedDB</code></td>
      <td>Holds your sign-in session token so you stay logged in between pages. Written only if you create an account
        and log in.</td>
      <td>Until you log out or the token expires.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>
  You can delete both at any time by clearing site data for tzortzoglou.eu in your browser settings. Clearing the theme
  key simply resets the site to its default appearance; clearing the Firebase entry logs you out.
</p>

<h2 id="choice">3. Your analytics choice</h2>

<p id="consent-state" class="font-semibold"></p>
<p>
  <button type="button" id="consent-withdraw" class="btn-secondary px-5 py-2 text-sm">Turn analytics on</button>
</p>
<p id="consent-note" role="status" aria-live="polite" class="text-sm"></p>
<noscript>
  <p><strong>JavaScript is disabled, so no analytics runs at all.</strong> Nothing is being counted.</p>
</noscript>

<p>
  You are asked once, on your first visit. <strong>Analytics stays off unless you actively accept</strong> — declining
  is one click, exactly as easy as accepting, and nothing on the site changes either way. Your answer is remembered in
  your browser under <code>analytics-consent</code> and never leaves your device.
</p>

<h3>Why the banner only asks about this one thing</h3>
<p>
  Under Article 5(3) of the ePrivacy Directive (in Sweden, the Electronic Communications Act,
  <span lang="sv">lag (2022:482) om elektronisk kommunikation</span>), consent is required before storing or reading
  information on your device — <em>except</em> where it is strictly necessary for a service you explicitly asked for.
</p>
<p>
  The theme preference and the login token both fall inside that exemption, so asking permission for them would be
  asking a question with only one honest answer. The page counter is different: it is not necessary for anything, so it
  is the one thing genuinely worth your consent, and the one thing the banner controls. A banner whose buttons changed
  nothing would be theatre, and regulators across the EU have repeatedly acted against exactly that pattern.
</p>
<p>
  Because analytics now runs on consent rather than on legitimate interest, you can also withdraw it at any time using
  the button above — as easily as you gave it (Art. 7(3) GDPR).
</p>
<p>
  If this site ever adds anything else that requires consent — advertising, cross-site analytics, an embedded YouTube or
  Maps frame, a marketing pixel — it will be added to this same mechanism <strong>before</strong> it goes live.
</p>

